    detailedExplanation: "Article 213 mirrors Article 123 at the State level, but incorporates specific federal checks through the Presidential instructions proviso.\n\n### 1. The Proviso: Presidential Instructions Check\nUnlike Article 123, Article 213 contains a crucial federal restraint: the Governor cannot bypass the Centre on sensitive topics. If an Ordinance impinges on High Court powers, violates a Central Act on the Concurrent List (Article 254), or requires presidential reservation under Article 200, the Governor MUST obtain prior instructions from the President of India before promulgation.\n\n### 2. The Bihar Ordinance Raj & D.C. Wadhwa (1987)\nIn the State of Bihar, the Executive created an 'Ordinance Raj' between 1967 and 1981, repeatedly re-promulgating 256 ordinances for up to 14 years without ever introducing them as Bills in the State Legislature. A Constitution Bench headed by Chief Justice P.N. Bhagwati struck down this practice as a **subversion of the democratic process and a fraud on the Constitution**.\n\n### 3. The Definitive Benchmark: Krishna Kumar Singh v. State of Bihar (2017)\nA 7-judge Constitution Bench comprehensively resolved the jurisprudence of Article 213:\n1. **Laying is Mandatory:** The requirement to lay the ordinance before the legislature under Article 213(2)(a) is mandatory. An executive that fails to lay an ordinance commits a grave constitutional violation.\n2. **Re-promulgation is a Fraud:** Re-promulgating ordinances without placing them before the legislature is a fraud on the Constitution and renders the re-promulgated ordinance void ab initio.\n3. **Judicial Review:** The satisfaction of the Governor under Article 213(1) is subject to judicial review. While courts do not substitute their subjective assessment, they examine whether the satisfaction is based on relevant material or tainted by mala fides.\n4. **Survival of Consequences:** An expired or disapproved ordinance does not confer permanent, irrevocable rights; rights created under it dissolve unless reversing them is impossible or contrary to public interest.",
