    detailedExplanation: "Article 246 is the legislative engine of Indian federalism, borrowing the three-list model from Section 100 of the Government of India Act, 1935.\n\n### 1. The Hierarchy of Non-Obstante Clauses\nThe opening phrases of Article 246 establish a clear hierarchy of federal supremacy:\n- Clause (1) begins: *'Notwithstanding anything in clauses (2) and (3)'* — giving List I absolute priority over Lists II and III.\n- Clause (2) begins: *'Notwithstanding anything in clause (3), but subject to clause (1)'* — giving Concurrent List priority over State List, but subordinating it to Union List.\n- Clause (3) begins: *'Subject to clauses (1) and (2)'* — making State List subordinate to both Union and Concurrent Lists.\nHowever, in *In re Central Provinces and Berar Act (1939)* and *State of Bombay v. F.N. Balsara (1951)*, the courts established that federal supremacy is a **measure of last resort**; courts must first make every effort to read the entries harmoniously.\n\n### 2. The Doctrine of Pith and Substance\nLegislative entries are not powers, but fields of legislation that must be construed broadly and generously (*Navinchandra Mafatlal*). When a statute enacted under one list incidentally touches upon a subject in another list, the court applies the **Doctrine of Pith and Substance**:\n- **Prafulla Kumar Mukherjee v. Bank of Commerce (1947):** The Bengal Moneylenders Act, 1940 enacted under State List ('moneylending') limited interest on promissory notes ('banking' and 'negotiable instruments' under Union List). The Privy Council held that the true nature and character (pith and substance) of the Act was moneylending; incidental encroachment on negotiable instruments did not invalidate it.\n- **State of Bombay v. F.N. Balsara (1951):** The Bombay Prohibition Act (State List 'intoxicating liquors') incidentally affected the import of liquor (Union List). The Supreme Court upheld the Act, ruling that its pith and substance was total prohibition within the State.\n\n### 3. Goods and Services Tax (Article 246A)\nThe 101st Constitutional Amendment Act, 2016 inserted **Article 246A**, introducing a revolutionary constitutional departure from the exclusive compartmentalization of Article 246. Article 246A confers **simultaneous, concurrent legislative power** on both Parliament and State Legislatures to make laws with respect to Goods and Services Tax, guided by the cooperative federalism of the GST Council (Article 279A).",
