    detailedExplanation: "Article 254 is the constitutional referee governing the shared Concurrent List.\n\n### 1. The Definitive Tests of Repugnancy\nIn *M. Karunanidhi v. Union of India (1979)*, a 5-judge Constitution Bench formulated the authoritative propositions on repugnancy:\n1. There must be a clear and direct inconsistency between the Central Act and the State Act.\n2. The inconsistency must be of such a nature that the two acts cannot stand together or be reconciled (obedience to one involves disobeying the other).\n3. If the Central Act occupies the entire field and exhibits an intention to be an exhaustive code, a State Act entering the same field is repugnant even if it does not directly contradict the Central text.\n4. Where two enactments operate in different fields without collision, there is no repugnancy.\n\n### 2. The Presidential Assent Exception (Article 254(2))\nClause (2) embodies asymmetric cooperative federalism:\n- A State can enact a law departing from an existing Central Act on a Concurrent matter (e.g., land acquisition amendments, consumer protection rules).\n- If the Governor reserves the Bill under Article 200 and the President grants assent under Article 201, the State law **prevails in that State** over the prior Central law.\n- **Kaiser-I-Hind Pvt. Ltd. v. National Textile Corp. (2002):** A Constitution Bench held that for Article 254(2) to operate, the State Government must have explicitly pointed out the specific Central law and the exact repugnancy to the President when seeking assent; a vague, general assent does not cure repugnancy.\n\n### 3. The Proviso: Ultimate Parliamentary Overrule\nEven after a State law receives Presidential assent under Clause (2), the federal balance tilts back to the Union: Parliament can at any time enact a fresh statute with respect to the same matter, directly adding to, amending, or overriding the State law (*Zaverbhai Amaidas v. State of Bombay, 1954*).",
