    detailedExplanation: "Article 300A marks the transition of property rights from an absolute barrier against land reforms into a regulated constitutional right.\n\n### 1. The Pre-1978 Tumultuous History\nOriginally, the right to property was fiercely protected under Part III:\n- **Article 19(1)(f):** Right to acquire, hold, and dispose of property;\n- **Article 31:** Compulsory acquisition requiring public purpose and compensation.\nThe judicial-parliamentary battle over compensation in land reforms led to repeated amendments (1st, 4th, 17th, 24th, 25th Amendments) and the Golaknath and Kesavananda crises.\nFinally, the **44th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1978** deleted Articles 19(1)(f) and 31 from Part III, and inserted **Article 300A** in Part XII as a constitutional right.\n\n### 2. Is Article 300A Toothless? The K.T. Plantation Benchmark\nLitigants argued that because Article 300A omitted the explicit mention of 'compensation' and 'public purpose' found in old Article 31, the State could acquire property without paying a single rupee.\nIn *K.T. Plantation Pvt. Ltd. v. State of Karnataka (2011)*, a 5-judge Constitution Bench rejected this view:\n1. The word **'law'** in Article 300A does not mean any arbitrary enactment; it must be a valid, just, fair, and reasonable law satisfying Article 14.\n2. The requirement of **public purpose** is an inherent facet of the sovereign power of eminent domain.\n3. The requirement of **compensation** (or indemnification) is a built-in element of any valid expropriatory legislation, although the quantum need not be market value in all socialist welfare measures.\n\n### 3. Property as a Human Right: Vidya Devi (2020) & Sukh Dutt Ratra (2022)\nIn *Vidya Devi v. State of Himachal Pradesh (2020)*, the State took over an illiterate widow's land for constructing a highway in 1967 without initiating land acquisition proceedings or paying compensation. Fifty years later, the Supreme Court castigated the State:\n> *'The right to property was a fundamental right until 1978 and continues to be an invaluable human right under Article 300A. The State cannot be permitted to act as a land-grabber taking private property without authority of law.'*\nThe Court ordered the State to pay full compensation along with solatium and litigation costs.",
